The Old, Hard Way (Without Active Directory):
When a new employee joins, the IT guy has to walk to that person’s laptop, create a local username and password, walk over to the file server, create another account there, and walk to the printer to set it up.

If that employee gets fired, the IT guy has to run around the whole building deleting their access from every single machine.

It is slow, messy, and impossible to manage.

The Smart Way (Active Directory):
Microsoft created Active Directory (AD).

Think of AD as one giant central computer (the Domain Controller) that holds the master list of everyone and everything in the entire company.

Instead of every laptop remembering passwords, every laptop simply calls the central server and asks: "Hey, this person is trying to type in their password—are they legit?"

If you hire or fire someone, you make one click on that central server, and they are added or removed across the whole company instantly.
